function fs_new_content_guides_2() {
    return [
    [
        'slug'    => 'negative-equity-car-loan-how-to-get-out',
        'title'   => 'Negative Equity on a Car Loan: How to Get Out',
        'keyword' => 'negative equity car loan',
        'desc'    => 'Owe more on your car than it is worth? Learn how to calculate negative equity, what rolling it into a new loan costs, and seven ways to get out of it.',
        'excerpt' => 'How negative equity on a car loan happens, what it costs to roll it into a new loan, and the best ways to get out of it.',
        'content' => '<p class="lead">You are upside down on a car loan when you owe more than the car is worth. It is one of the most common money traps for car buyers, and it gets more expensive the longer you ignore it. This guide explains how to work out your exact number, what happens if you trade the car in, and seven ways to get out. You can run your own figures in our <a href="{tool:negative-equity-car-loan-calculator}">negative equity car loan calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What negative equity means</h2><p>Equity is the difference between what the car is worth and what you still owe. If the car is worth $12,000 and you owe $16,000, your equity is minus $4,000. That minus $4,000 is negative equity, also called being upside down or underwater. If you sold the car today, the sale price would not even cover the loan, and you would owe the lender the shortfall out of your own pocket.</p>'
            . '<p>Negative equity is not a sign that you did something wrong, and it is not a problem by itself while you keep the car. It becomes a problem when you want to sell or trade the car, when it is totaled or stolen, or when you want to refinance.</p>'
            . '<h2>Why car loans go underwater</h2><ul><li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> A new car commonly loses a large share of its value in the first year, often around 20%, and keeps losing value every year after that. Exact figures depend on the model, mileage and condition.</li><li><strong>Small or no down payment.</strong> If you borrow the full price plus tax and fees, the loan starts above the car’s value on day one.</li><li><strong>Long loan terms.</strong> On a 72- or 84-month loan, early payments go mostly to interest, so the balance falls slowly while the car’s value falls fast.</li><li><strong>High interest rates.</strong> A higher APR means less of each payment reduces the principal.</li><li><strong>Rolled-in debt.</strong> Adding the balance from a previous car to a new loan starts you underwater by that amount.</li><li><strong>Add-ons.</strong> Extended warranties, service contracts and other extras financed into the loan add to what you owe without adding resale value.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How to calculate your negative equity</h2><ol><li><strong>Get your payoff amount.</strong> Ask your lender for the exact payoff figure for today. It is slightly higher than the balance on your statement because it includes interest accrued since the last payment.</li><li><strong>Find your car’s trade-in value.</strong> Use a valuation guide such as Kelley Blue Book, Edmunds or NADA as a starting point, then get real offers from a few dealers or online car buyers. Use the trade-in or wholesale figure, not the private-sale price.</li><li><strong>Subtract.</strong> Trade-in value minus payoff amount. A negative result is your negative equity.</li></ol><p>Example: your car’s trade-in value is $12,000 and the payoff is $16,000. Your negative equity is $4,000.</p>'
            . '<h2>What if you have positive equity?</h2><p>Sometimes the answer is the reverse. Suppose you want a $21,000 car, your current car is worth $19,500 and you owe $14,500. Your positive equity is $19,500 − $14,500 = $5,000. That $5,000 works like a down payment, so you would need to borrow about $16,000 plus tax and fees, instead of the full $21,000. The calculator handles both cases: enter a trade-in value higher than the payoff and it subtracts your equity from the new loan.</p>'
            . '<h2>What rolling negative equity into a new loan really costs</h2><p>Dealers often say they can "take care of" your negative equity. What they usually do is add it to the new loan. Here is what that looks like with the numbers from the calculator:</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Without the old debt</th><th>With $4,000 rolled in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>New car price</td><td>$28,000</td><td>$28,000</td></tr><tr><td>Down payment, tax (6%) and fees</td><td>$2,000 down, $1,680 tax, $500 fees</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>Loan amount</td><td>$28,180</td><td>$32,180</td></tr><tr><td>Loan as a share of the car’s price</td><td>about 101%</td><td>about 115%</td></tr><tr><td>Payment at 7.5% for 72 months</td><td>$487.24</td><td>$556.40</td></tr><tr><td>Extra over the loan</td><td>-</td><td>about $4,980</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>The $4,000 of old debt costs about $980 in extra interest, for roughly $4,980 in additional payments, and you begin the new loan owing 15% more than the car’s price. Because you are underwater by even more, the cycle repeats at the next trade-in. Many lenders also cap how much they will lend compared with a car’s value, so a large gap may reduce your options or raise your rate.</p>'
            . '<h2>Seven ways to get out of negative equity</h2><h3>1. Keep the car and keep paying</h3><p>If the car runs well and the payment fits your budget, the simplest fix is to keep driving it. The balance falls over time, and depreciation slows as the car ages, so the gap closes on its own, usually in the later years of the loan.</p>'
            . '<h3>2. Pay extra toward the principal</h3><p>Even $50 to $100 more a month, applied to principal, closes the gap faster and cuts interest. Tell your lender that extra amounts should go to principal, not the next payment. The <a href="{tool:loan-payoff-calculator}">loan payoff calculator</a> shows how much time and interest extra payments save.</p>'
            . '<h3>3. Make a lump-sum payment</h3><p>A tax refund, bonus or savings can wipe out much of the gap at once. A $2,000 lump sum on a $16,000 balance saves interest on that $2,000 for the rest of the loan.</p>'
            . '<h3>4. Refinance, if it lowers your rate</h3><p>Refinancing does not erase negative equity, because the new lender still wants the full balance, and many will not refinance a loan that is well above the car’s value. But if your credit has improved and you can get a lower rate, more of each payment goes to principal. Use the <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a> to check whether a lower rate would actually save money.</p>'
            . '<h3>5. Pay the shortfall in cash when you trade</h3><p>If you must replace the car, paying the gap in cash at the dealer keeps the new loan clean. It hurts now but avoids paying interest on the old debt for years.</p>'
            . '<h3>6. Sell the car privately</h3><p>Private buyers typically pay more than a dealer’s trade-in offer. A higher sale price can shrink the shortfall. You will need to pay off the loan at the sale, so coordinate with your lender about releasing the title.</p>'
            . '<h3>7. Roll it over only as a last resort</h3><p>If you must roll it into a new loan, limit the damage: choose a cheaper car, put down as much cash as you can, pick the shortest term you can afford, and get an outside loan quote to compare with the dealer’s financing. Our <a href="{tool:negative-equity-car-loan-calculator}">negative equity calculator</a> shows the cost before you sign.</p>'
            . '<h2>What about GAP insurance?</h2><p>Guaranteed asset protection (GAP) insurance pays the difference between what you owe and the car’s value if it is totaled or stolen. It is worth considering when you have a small down payment or a long loan. It does not cover the shortfall when you trade the car in, and it is often cheaper through your own insurer or credit union than through the dealer.</p>'
            . '<h2>Questions to ask the dealer or lender</h2><ul><li>What is the exact payoff amount, and when does it expire?</li><li>What is the loan-to-value limit on the new loan, and how does the rate change if the loan is above the car’s value?</li><li>What is the total amount financed, including the old balance, taxes, fees and any add-ons?</li><li>Can the shortfall be paid in cash instead of financed?</li><li>What would the payment be for a shorter term?</li></ul><p>Ask for the numbers in writing, and compare them with an outside loan quote. A dealer who only talks about the monthly payment may be hiding how much you are borrowing.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common myths about negative equity</h2><ul><li><strong>"It disappears when you trade in."</strong> It does not. It moves to the new loan.</li><li><strong>"GAP insurance covers it."</strong> GAP covers a total loss, not a trade-in.</li><li><strong>"A lower payment means a better deal."</strong> Stretching the term lowers the payment but increases interest and keeps you underwater longer.</li><li><strong>"Refinancing erases it."</strong> A refinance can lower the rate, but the debt is still there.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How to avoid it next time</h2><ul><li>Put down at least 10% to 20% of the price.</li><li>Choose a loan term of 60 months or less if you can afford the payment.</li><li>Do not finance add-ons you can pay for separately or skip.</li><li>Shop for a loan before you shop for the car. Use the <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a> to find a payment you can afford, and compare incentives with the <a href="{tool:cash-back-vs-low-apr-calculator}">cash back vs low APR calculator</a>.</li><li>Buy a car with a strong resale value, or consider a gently used car that has already taken the biggest depreciation hit.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Checklist before you trade in</h2><ol><li>Get the 10-day payoff amount from your lender.</li><li>Get at least three real offers for your car.</li><li>Calculate your equity.</li><li>Decide how much of any shortfall you can pay in cash.</li><li>Get a pre-approved loan quote to compare with the dealer’s.</li><li>Check the total cost, not just the monthly payment.</li></ol>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and does not replace advice from a licensed professional. Loan terms and valuation vary by lender and location.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'    => [
            [ 'What is negative equity on a car loan?', 'It means you owe more on the loan than the car is currently worth. For example, owing $16,000 on a car worth $12,000 is $4,000 of negative equity.' ],
            [ 'How do I calculate negative equity?', 'Subtract your loan payoff amount from the car’s trade-in value. If the result is below zero, that is your negative equity.' ],
            [ 'Can I trade in a car with negative equity?', 'Yes, but the dealer typically adds the shortfall to your new loan unless you pay it in cash. That raises your loan amount, payment and total interest.' ],
            [ 'How much negative equity can I roll into a new loan?', 'It depends on the lender, which usually limits the loan compared with the car’s value, and on your credit. Even if a lender allows it, rolling in a large amount is expensive.' ],
            [ 'Does refinancing fix negative equity?', 'Not by itself. A lower rate can reduce interest and speed up paying down the balance, but the new lender still needs the full loan amount, and many will not refinance a loan far above the car’s value.' ],
            [ 'Will GAP insurance pay off negative equity when I trade in?', 'No. GAP insurance only pays the gap if the car is totaled or stolen.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'    => 'cash-back-or-low-apr-car-deal',
        'title'   => 'Cash Back or Low APR? How to Pick the Better Car Deal',
        'keyword' => 'cash back or low apr',
        'desc'    => 'Should you take the rebate or the low-APR financing on a new car? See the math, a worked example, the break-even rate and when each option wins.',
        'excerpt' => 'How to compare a manufacturer cash rebate with a low promotional APR, with a worked example and a simple break-even rule.',
        'content' => '<p class="lead">New-car ads love to promise "$3,000 cash back or 1.9% financing." You usually cannot have both, so which one saves more money? The answer depends on three numbers: the size of the rebate, the promotional rate, and the rate you could get on your own. This guide shows how to compare them in a few minutes, and you can test your numbers in the <a href="{tool:cash-back-vs-low-apr-calculator}">cash back vs low APR calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>The two offers explained</h2><ul><li><strong>Cash back (rebate).</strong> A manufacturer discount that lowers the price you finance. Take $3,000 off a $35,000 car and you borrow $3,000 less.</li><li><strong>Low APR (promotional financing).</strong> A below-market interest rate, sometimes 0%, offered through the manufacturer’s lender for qualified buyers. You borrow the full price, but pay little or no interest.</li></ul><p>Manufacturers frequently make these choices either-or. If you take the rebate, you finance at a normal rate, which you can often get from your own bank or credit union.</p>'
            . '<h2>The comparison rule in plain English</h2><p>Add up everything you will pay under each option and pick the smaller total. In short:</p><ol><li><strong>Option A (rebate):</strong> loan = price − rebate − down payment, at the rate you can get on your own.</li><li><strong>Option B (low APR):</strong> loan = price − down payment, at the promotional rate.</li><li>Compare total payments over the term. The cheaper total wins.</li></ol><p>The monthly payment matters for your budget, but total cost shows which deal really saves money.</p>'
            . '<h2>A worked example</h2><p>A $35,000 car, $3,000 down, 60 months. Option A is a $3,000 rebate with a 6.5% loan from your own credit union. Option B is a 1.9% dealer rate with no rebate.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>A: rebate + 6.5% loan</th><th>B: 1.9% dealer financing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Amount financed</td><td>$29,000</td><td>$32,000</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly payment</td><td>$567.42</td><td>$559.49</td></tr><tr><td>Total of payments (60 months)</td><td>$34,045</td><td>$33,569</td></tr><tr><td>Interest paid</td><td>about $5,045</td><td>about $1,569</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Here the low APR wins by about $476, and it also has the lower monthly payment. The reason is that on a $29,000 to $32,000 loan, a 4.6-point lower rate saves more interest than the $3,000 rebate removes.</p>'
            . '<h2>Find the break-even rate</h2><p>You can flip the question: at what promotional rate do the two options cost the same? In this example the break-even promotional rate is about 2.46%. If the dealer’s rate is lower than that, take the low APR. If it is higher, take the rebate. At a 4.5% promotional rate, the rebate would win by about $1,749.</p><p>The calculator finds the break-even rate for you each time you change the numbers.</p>'
            . '<h2>How the answer shifts: four tables</h2><p>All figures use the same $35,000 car and $3,000 down payment, and compare total payments. Option A is the rebate with your own loan; Option B is the dealer’s low rate.</p>'
            . '<h3>If the promotional rate changes (60 months, $3,000 rebate, your rate 6.5%)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Promo APR</th><th>Total, low APR (B)</th><th>Total, rebate (A)</th><th>Cheaper</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0%</td><td>$32,000</td><td>$34,045</td><td>Low APR by $2,045</td></tr><tr><td>1.9%</td><td>$33,569</td><td>$34,045</td><td>Low APR by $476</td></tr><tr><td>2.9%</td><td>$34,415</td><td>$34,045</td><td>Rebate by $370</td></tr><tr><td>3.9%</td><td>$35,273</td><td>$34,045</td><td>Rebate by $1,228</td></tr><tr><td>4.9%</td><td>$36,145</td><td>$34,045</td><td>Rebate by $2,100</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h3>If the rebate changes (60 months, promo 1.9%, your rate 6.5%)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Rebate</th><th>Total, rebate (A)</th><th>Total, low APR (B)</th><th>Cheaper</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$1,000</td><td>$36,393</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Low APR by $2,824</td></tr><tr><td>$2,000</td><td>$35,219</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Low APR by $1,650</td></tr><tr><td>$3,000</td><td>$34,045</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Low APR by $476</td></tr><tr><td>$4,000</td><td>$32,871</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Rebate by $698</td></tr><tr><td>$5,000</td><td>$31,697</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Rebate by $1,872</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h3>If the loan term changes ($3,000 rebate, promo 1.9%, your rate 6.5%)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Total, rebate (A)</th><th>Total, low APR (B)</th><th>Cheaper</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>36 months</td><td>$31,998</td><td>$32,946</td><td>Rebate by $948</td></tr><tr><td>48 months</td><td>$33,011</td><td>$33,257</td><td>Rebate by $246</td></tr><tr><td>60 months</td><td>$34,045</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Low APR by $476</td></tr><tr><td>72 months</td><td>$35,099</td><td>$33,884</td><td>Low APR by $1,215</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h3>If your own loan rate changes ($3,000 rebate, promo 1.9%, 60 months)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Your rate</th><th>Total, rebate (A)</th><th>Total, low APR (B)</th><th>Cheaper</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4.5%</td><td>$32,439</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Rebate by $1,131</td></tr><tr><td>5.5%</td><td>$33,236</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Rebate by $333</td></tr><tr><td>6.5%</td><td>$34,045</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Low APR by $476</td></tr><tr><td>8.5%</td><td>$35,699</td><td>$33,569</td><td>Low APR by $2,130</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Three patterns show up. The rebate gets better as it gets bigger and as your own rate gets lower. The low rate gets better as the loan gets longer and as your own rate gets higher. When the totals are close, other factors, such as the monthly payment and the term, can decide it.</p>'
            . '<h2>Three buyers, three answers</h2><ul><li><strong>The buyer with a credit union pre-approval at 4.5%.</strong> With a $3,000 rebate, a 1.9% promo and a 60-month term, the rebate saves about $1,131. A good outside rate makes the rebate attractive.</li><li><strong>The buyer with average credit at 8.5%.</strong> The same offers favor the low APR by about $2,130, but only if this buyer qualifies for the promotional rate. If not, the rebate is the only real choice.</li><li><strong>The buyer who wants a 36-month loan.</strong> On a short term, the rebate saves about $948, because there is less interest for the low rate to save.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>What changes the answer</h2><h3>The rate you can get on your own</h3><p>If your credit union offers 4%, the rebate becomes more attractive, because the gap between its rate and the dealer’s is smaller. If your own rate is 9% or higher, the low APR looks better. Always get a pre-approval before you shop, so you know your real alternative.</p>'
            . '<h3>The loan term</h3><p>Interest accumulates over time, so a long loan makes a low rate more valuable. A 72-month loan at 1.9% saves more than a 36-month loan at 1.9%, compared with the same loan at a normal rate. On short loans, a rebate often wins.</p>'
            . '<h3>The size of the rebate</h3><p>A $500 rebate does little. A $5,000 rebate is hard to beat unless the promotional rate is 0% and the loan is large and long. Run the numbers instead of guessing.</p>'
            . '<h3>Credit requirements</h3><p>Promotional rates are generally reserved for borrowers with very good credit and may be limited to certain models and shorter terms. If you are not approved for the advertised rate, the rebate may be the only deal you can actually get.</p>'
            . '<h3>Sales tax</h3><p>In some states sales tax is calculated on the price before the rebate, in others after. That changes the amount you finance a little. Check how your state treats rebates.</p>'
            . '<h2>Used cars, leases and other special cases</h2><p>Manufacturer incentives are usually offered on new vehicles. Some brands also offer special rates on certified pre-owned cars, which can make them worth comparing, but the rebate-versus-rate choice is mostly a new-car question. Lease deals work differently: the advertised payment depends on the money factor, the residual value and any cash due at signing, so a rebate-versus-APR comparison does not apply. If you are choosing between buying and leasing, compare the total cost over the time you plan to keep the car, not just the monthly payment.</p><p>Also watch out for the fees around the deal. Documentation fees, dealer add-ons, extended warranties and service contracts can add thousands to the amount financed. The comparison above assumes the same fees under both options, so check that neither offer comes with extra charges, such as a required add-on or a higher price.</p>'
            . '<h2>Steps to take before you sign</h2><ol><li><strong>Get pre-approved</strong> by your bank or credit union and write down the rate and term.</li><li><strong>Negotiate the price first.</strong> Agree on the car’s price before discussing rebates or financing, so the incentives are not used to hide a higher price.</li><li><strong>Ask for both offers in writing</strong>, including any conditions.</li><li><strong>Run the numbers</strong> in the <a href="{tool:cash-back-vs-low-apr-calculator}">calculator</a> with the same down payment and term for both.</li><li><strong>Check the total cost</strong>, not just the monthly payment, and avoid stretching the term to lower the payment.</li><li><strong>Read the fine print</strong> on the low-APR offer: credit score minimums, term limits and whether the rebate can be combined with other incentives.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Avoid these mistakes</h2><ul><li>Choosing based on the monthly payment alone. A longer term can lower the payment and raise the total cost.</li><li>Letting the dealer set the loan before you know your own rate.</li><li>Rolling negative equity from your old car into the new loan. See our guide to <a href="/negative-equity-car-loan-how-to-get-out/">getting out of negative equity</a> and use the <a href="{tool:negative-equity-car-loan-calculator}">negative equity calculator</a> first.</li><li>Financing add-ons such as extended warranties into the loan without comparing prices.</li><li>Ignoring the down payment. A larger down payment lowers the amount financed under either option, and can reduce the dealer’s fees too.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Quick rules of thumb</h2><ul><li>Large rebate, short term, strong outside rate: the <strong>rebate</strong> often wins.</li><li>Small rebate, long term, very low or 0% promo rate: the <strong>low APR</strong> often wins.</li><li>Close call: pick the option with the lower monthly payment you are comfortable with, or the one with the shorter term.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>For a full picture of what your car payment will be, use the <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a>, and compare lenders with the <a href="{tool:loan-comparison-calculator}">loan comparison calculator</a>. This guide is educational and not financial advice. Offers and eligibility vary by manufacturer, lender and location.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'    => [
            [ 'Is cash back or low APR better?', 'Compare total cost. Take the low APR when the promotional rate is below the break-even rate, and the rebate when it is above. A larger rebate, shorter term and a good outside rate favor the rebate.' ],
            [ 'Can I take the rebate and the low APR together?', 'Sometimes, but many manufacturers make them either-or. Ask the dealer and read the offer’s terms.' ],
            [ 'Is 0% financing always the best deal?', 'No. A large rebate paired with a reasonable outside loan can cost less than 0% financing with no rebate.' ],
            [ 'Who qualifies for the promotional rate?', 'Usually borrowers with very good credit, and often only for certain models and terms.' ],
            [ 'Should I get my own financing before going to the dealer?', 'Yes. A pre-approval shows your real alternative rate and gives you leverage when comparing offers.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'    => 'debt-avalanche-vs-snowball',
        'title'   => 'Debt Avalanche vs Snowball: Which Pays Off Debt Faster?',
        'keyword' => 'debt avalanche vs snowball',
        'desc'    => 'Debt avalanche or debt snowball? See how each method works, a side-by-side example with real numbers, and how to choose the one you will actually finish.',
        'excerpt' => 'How the debt avalanche and debt snowball methods work, a worked comparison, and how to pick the right one for you.',
        'content' => '<p class="lead">Both methods get you out of debt. The debt avalanche saves the most money, and the debt snowball gives you quick wins that keep you motivated. This guide compares them with the same set of debts, so you can see the real difference in months and dollars. Test your own balances in our <a href="{tool:loan-payoff-calculator}">loan payoff calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>How both methods work</h2><p>The two methods share the same foundation. You pay the minimum on every debt, then put every spare dollar toward one target debt. When that debt is gone, you roll its payment (the old minimum plus the extra) onto the next debt. The only difference is how you choose the target.</p>'
            . '<ul><li><strong>Debt avalanche:</strong> target the debt with the <strong>highest interest rate</strong> first, no matter the balance. It costs the least in interest.</li><li><strong>Debt snowball:</strong> target the debt with the <strong>smallest balance</strong> first, no matter the rate. You clear whole debts sooner, which feels like progress.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>A side-by-side example</h2><p>Here are three debts that add up to $12,000, plus $300 a month of extra money on top of the minimums, for a total budget of $665 a month.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Debt</th><th>Balance</th><th>Rate (APR)</th><th>Minimum payment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Store card</td><td>$1,500</td><td>18%</td><td>$45</td></tr><tr><td>Personal loan</td><td>$4,000</td><td>9%</td><td>$120</td></tr><tr><td>Credit card</td><td>$6,500</td><td>27%</td><td>$200</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Running each method month by month, with the same $665 paid every month until everything is gone, the results are:</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Snowball (smallest balance first)</th><th>Avalanche (highest rate first)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Order of payoff</td><td>Store card, personal loan, credit card</td><td>Credit card, store card, personal loan</td></tr><tr><td>First debt cleared</td><td>Month 5</td><td>Month 16</td></tr><tr><td>Debt-free</td><td>Month 23</td><td>Month 22</td></tr><tr><td>Total interest</td><td>about $2,762</td><td>about $2,077</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>The avalanche saves about $685 in interest and finishes one month earlier. But the snowball clears its first debt in month 5, while the avalanche takes 16 months to clear its first one. For some people that gap in early progress matters more than the money.</p>'
            . '<p>For comparison, paying only the fixed minimums on these debts would take about 50 months and cost about $6,166 in interest. Either method beats that by a wide margin. The extra payment is what really matters.</p>'
            . '<h2>Pros and cons</h2><h3>Debt avalanche</h3><ul><li><strong>Pros:</strong> lowest total interest, usually the fastest payoff, and it is the mathematically best choice.</li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> if your highest-rate debt is also your biggest, you may go a long time without a visible win.</li></ul><h3>Debt snowball</h3><ul><li><strong>Pros:</strong> early wins, fewer accounts to manage sooner, and a lift in motivation.</li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> you pay more interest, because high-rate debts stay open longer.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How to choose</h2><ol><li><strong>Look at your rates.</strong> If the highest rate is much higher than the rest (like 27% against 9%), the avalanche saves a lot. If your rates are all close together, the snowball costs very little extra.</li><li><strong>Be honest about motivation.</strong> A plan you abandon in month 6 saves nothing. Some behavioral research suggests that early wins help people stick with a payoff plan, but the best method is the one you will finish.</li><li><strong>Check the balances.</strong> If your smallest balance is also your highest rate, both methods give the same order.</li><li><strong>Consider a hybrid.</strong> Start with one or two small debts to build momentum, then switch to the avalanche for the rest.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>How to start: a 6-step plan</h2><ol><li><strong>List every debt</strong> with its balance, rate and minimum. Include credit cards, personal loans, car loans and student loans.</li><li><strong>Find your extra money.</strong> Review your spending with the <a href="{tool:monthly-budget-planner}">monthly budget planner</a> and see how much you can add above the minimums.</li><li><strong>Pick a method</strong> and rank your debts.</li><li><strong>Automate the minimums</strong> on every debt so you never miss a payment, and send the extra to your target.</li><li><strong>Roll the payment forward.</strong> When a debt is cleared, add its whole payment to the next target.</li><li><strong>Track progress monthly</strong> and update the plan if your income, rates or balances change.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Can you lower the rate first?</h2><p>Either method works better with a lower interest rate. Two options are worth checking before you start:</p><ul><li><strong>A balance transfer card</strong> with a 0% introductory rate can pause interest on card debt for a time. Use the <a href="{tool:balance-transfer-calculator}">balance transfer calculator</a> to check that the fee is worth it and that you can pay the balance before the promotional period ends.</li><li><strong>A consolidation loan</strong> rolls several debts into one payment, often at a lower fixed rate. Compare it with the <a href="{tool:debt-consolidation-calculator}">debt consolidation calculator</a>, and read how it can affect your credit in our guide on <a href="/does-debt-consolidation-hurt-your-credit/">whether debt consolidation hurts your credit</a>.</li></ul><p>If you consolidate, you can still use the snowball or avalanche idea by paying extra on the new loan.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li><strong>Skipping the emergency cushion.</strong> Without a small buffer, a surprise bill goes back on a credit card. Build a starter fund of $500 to $1,000 first, and see how big your full emergency fund should be with our <a href="{tool:emergency-fund-calculator}">emergency fund calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Missing a minimum payment.</strong> Late fees and penalty rates can erase your progress.</li><li><strong>Adding new debt.</strong> Stop using the cards you are paying off, or the total never shrinks.</li><li><strong>Ignoring fixed-rate loans.</strong> A low-rate loan like a mortgage or federal student loan usually belongs at the end of the list.</li><li><strong>Giving up after a setback.</strong> One bad month does not break the plan. Restart the next month.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Why the interest rate matters so much</h2><p>Credit card interest is usually charged monthly on the balance. A 27% APR means about 2.25% of the balance is added each month. On a $6,500 balance that is roughly $146 of interest in the first month, which is most of a $200 minimum payment. A 9% loan on $4,000 adds only about $30 a month. That is why a dollar of extra payment saves more when it goes to the high-rate card: it stops the biggest source of interest first.</p><p>It also shows why paying only the minimum is so slow. When most of the payment goes to interest, the balance barely moves, and the debt lasts for years.</p>'
            . '<h2>What if your highest rate is also your biggest balance?</h2><p>This is the case where the methods feel different. Imagine a $9,000 card at 26% and a $1,200 card at 15%. The avalanche pays the $9,000 card first, so it may take over a year before any account is closed. The snowball clears the $1,200 card in a few months, then moves on. The avalanche still saves more interest, but you will not see an account disappear for a long time. If that would make you quit, start with the snowball for the small card and switch once it is gone.</p>'
            . '<h2>Special cases to know about</h2><ul><li><strong>0% promotional balances.</strong> Do not put extra money on a 0% balance while a high-rate card exists. Pay the minimum on the 0% debt, and clear it before the promotion ends.</li><li><strong>Student loans.</strong> Federal loans have protections such as income-driven repayment, so many people treat them as lower priority than high-rate card debt. See our guide to <a href="/rap-vs-tiered-standard-student-loan-plan/">the new RAP and Tiered Standard repayment plans</a> before making extra payments.</li><li><strong>Secured loans.</strong> Car and home loans are backed by the asset, so missing payments has bigger consequences. Always keep them current.</li><li><strong>Variable rates.</strong> If a rate can change, re-check your order every few months.</li><li><strong>Fees.</strong> Some loans charge prepayment penalties. Read your loan terms before sending large extra payments.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Ways to stay motivated for a year or more</h2><ul><li><strong>Make progress visible.</strong> Keep a simple chart or spreadsheet, and mark each balance as it falls.</li><li><strong>Celebrate small milestones.</strong> Plan a free or inexpensive reward when each debt is cleared.</li><li><strong>Use windfalls.</strong> Send tax refunds, bonuses and gifts to the target debt. A $1,500 refund is worth several months of extra payments.</li><li><strong>Raise the extra amount over time.</strong> Each raise or expense you cut can be added to your payoff budget.</li><li><strong>Find a partner.</strong> Tell a friend or family member about your goal, or follow the plan together.</li></ul><p>Pair the plan with a budget you can keep. If you are not sure where your money goes, the <a href="{tool:monthly-budget-planner}">monthly budget planner</a> can help you find the first extra $50 to $100.</p>'
            . '<h2>Which one should you pick?</h2><p>If you want the cheapest route and you can stay focused for a year or more, use the avalanche. If you need early wins to keep going, use the snowball, or start there and switch. In the example above the gap was $685 and one month, which is small compared with the benefit of actually finishing. Run your own numbers in the <a href="{tool:loan-payoff-calculator}">loan payoff calculator</a>, then pick the plan you will stick with.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is for education and is not personal financial advice. Rates, fees and terms vary by lender.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'    => [
            [ 'What is the difference between the debt avalanche and the debt snowball?', 'The avalanche targets the highest interest rate first and saves the most interest. The snowball targets the smallest balance first and gives quicker wins. Both use the same extra payment.' ],
            [ 'Which is faster, avalanche or snowball?', 'The avalanche is usually faster or equal and always costs the least in interest. In the example here it finished one month sooner and saved about $685, though the snowball cleared its first debt 11 months earlier.' ],
            [ 'Should I pay off the smallest debt first?', 'It can help if you need motivation. If your smallest balance is not your highest rate, you will pay more interest than with the avalanche.' ],
            [ 'Can I combine both methods?', 'Yes. Many people clear one or two small debts first for momentum, then switch to the highest-rate-first order.' ],
            [ 'Does paying off debt this way help my credit score?', 'Paying down card balances lowers your credit utilization, which can help your score. Closing accounts after paying them off can reduce your available credit, so consider keeping old cards open.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
